  <article>
    <h2 class="post-title"><a href="/2026/09/07/making-machine-reservations.html">making machine reservations</a></h2>
    <p><time datetime="2026-09-07">07 September 2026</time></p>
    <p>The following Ansible script makes multiple machines reservations in a GCP project based on machine type, zone and running instances.</p>

<ul>
  <li>Create new reservations if non-existent.</li>
  <li>Update an existing reservation based on count.</li>
  <li>Delete a reservation if no instances are present.</li>
  <li>Print a summary at end of the script.</li>
</ul>


  </article>

  <article>
    <h2 class="post-title"><a href="/2026/05/15/column-t.html">column t</a></h2>
    <p><time datetime="2026-05-15">15 May 2026</time></p>
    <p>If you have a text file with multiple columns, you can use column to align the output vertically.</p>

<div class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code>cat output.txt | column -t 
</code></pre></div></div>


  </article>

  <article>
    <h2 class="post-title"><a href="/2026/05/05/run-command-recursively-directory.html">run command recursively directory</a></h2>
    <p><time datetime="2026-05-05">05 May 2026</time></p>
    <p>Here’s the script that will the dos2unix command recursively in the current directory.</p>

<p>It doesn’t have to be dos2unix but can be any command.</p>

<p>Run it on all files.</p>

<div class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code>find . -type f -exec dos2unix {} +
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Run it on all shell files only.</p>

<div class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code>find . -type f -name "*.sh" -exec dos2unix {} +
</code></pre></div></div>

  </article>

