  <article>
    <h2 class="post-title"><a href="/2025/12/09/aws-efs-tls-stunnel.html">aws efs tls stunnel</a></h2>
    <p><time datetime="2025-12-09">09 December 2025</time></p>
    <p>AWS EFS supports stunnel-based TLS encryption for NFS mounts.</p>

<p>Make sure distro has efs-utils installed.</p>

<div class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code>sudo yum install -y amazon-efs-utils
# OR
sudo apt-get install -y amazon-efs-utils
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>nfs-utils will mount drives but it will not provide encryption.</p>

<div class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code>sudo yum install nfs-utils
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Standard mount with TLS</p>

<div class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code>sudo mkdir -p /mnt/efs 
sudo mount -t efs -o tls fs-12345678:/ /mnt/efs 
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Mount via /etc/fstab</p>

<div class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code>fs-12345678:/ /mnt/efs efs _netdev,tls 0 0 
10.10.10.10:/ /mnt/efs efs _netdev,tls 0 0 
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Mount automatically</p>

<div class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code>sudo mount -a
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Check whether stunnel or efs-proxy is running</p>

<div class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code>ps aux | grep -E "stunnel|efs-proxy"
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>aws-efs-utils in not avaiable on RedHat 6 &amp; 7.</p>

  </article>

  <article>
    <h2 class="post-title"><a href="/2025/12/08/view-zipped-file-without-extraction.html">view zipped file without extraction</a></h2>
    <p><time datetime="2025-12-08">08 December 2025</time></p>
    <p>Here’s the command to view a zipped file without extraction.</p>

<div class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code>unzip -l file.zip
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Perfect for peeking what’s inside a zipped file without unpacking it.</p>

  </article>

  <article>
    <h2 class="post-title"><a href="/2025/12/08/two-column-page.html">two column page</a></h2>
    <p><time datetime="2025-12-08">08 December 2025</time></p>
    <p>Here’s some CSS to turn your site into a two-column page.</p>

<p>I’m using markdown language to list items. To render properly, markdown div is required.</p>

<div class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code>&lt;div class="multicolumn"&gt;
&lt;div class="column"&gt;
&lt;div markdown="1"&gt;
- link a
- link b
- link c
&lt;/div&gt; &lt;!-- close markdown --&gt;
&lt;/div&gt; &lt;!-- close column --&gt;
&lt;div class="column"&gt;
&lt;div markdown="1"&gt;
- link d
- link e
- link f
&lt;/div&gt; &lt;!-- close markdown --&gt;
&lt;/div&gt; &lt;!-- close column --&gt;
&lt;/div&gt; &lt;!-- close multicolumn --&gt;
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Finally the CSS. It will display 2 columns if browser is greater than 1080. Otherwise it collapses to one column.</p>

<div class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code>.multicolumn {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 20px;
}
.column {
  flex: 1 1 45%;
}
@media (max-width: 1080px) {
  .column {
    flex: 1 1 100%;
  }
}
</code></pre></div></div>

  </article>

  <article>
